WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.710
Film Courage: So then an example of a flat
character versus a more 3-dimensional character,

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.510
like even in just a description of something.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:11.880
Can you give me one sentence that is flat
versus one that tells you volumes in this

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:12.880
one sentence?

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:13.880
Karl Iglesias: Right.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:14.880
So you see this a lot.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.769
This is more about cliché writing so you’ve
got a character description where I read this

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:22.829
all the time “Oh, she’s beautiful or handsome!”

00:00:22.829 --> 00:00:25.009
You know, it’s the same…

00:00:25.009 --> 00:00:26.099
Film Courage: It tells me nothing.

00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:27.099
Karl Iglesias: It tells you nothing, right!

00:00:27.099 --> 00:00:29.119
It’s the same old thing you’ve seen a
hundred times.

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:30.589
So you’ve got to be a little more creative.

00:00:30.589 --> 00:00:32.410
It’s all about creativity and originality.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:36.100
I think originality comes from being curious.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.910
The more curious you are about something,
the more you know about something, the more

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:43.920
you can avoid copying something, right?

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.289
I think the people who have cliché writing
because they don't know what’s been done

00:00:49.289 --> 00:00:52.760
before, so to them it’s new so they go “Oh!”
because they think it’s good.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:58.399
But people who know and have seen it a hundred
times, because they’re curious, will say

00:00:58.399 --> 00:01:00.829
it’s cliché because they’ve seen it a
hundred times.

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:06.660
Film Courage: Versus a character description
of somebody that says “His eyes sparkled…”

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.260
I mean…oh…that is so cliché.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:10.820
Karl Iglesias: Right.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:11.820
It’s the whole cliché thing, right.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.300
They just don’t bother to go deeper.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.500
You know what I mean?

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.010
You’ve got to be original.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:20.330
Originality is very important because if you
read the same thing over and over again, you’re

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.770
not going to respond to it.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:28.080
That is actually the bane of a writer’s
existence, especially a screenwriter, to come

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:29.740
up with something new.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:36.590
When you think about, I think in the second
edition of 101 Habits, Bill Marsilii one of

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:40.920
the screenwriters there, talks about doing
the 20’s.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:47.270
So for him anything that he thinks about,
any event, any scene, character whatever,

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:51.120
he thinks of kind of listing 20 different
ways of doing it.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.750
He talks about the meet-cute in romantic comedy,
he talks about how people meet in a cute way.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:59.390
Well he’s going to do 20 different thoughts
about it.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.780
“Okay it could be this, it could be this…”

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.980
He does 20 because the first two or three
or five of them will be clichés.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:10.970
They will be things that you’re automatically
going to think about because they’re the

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.610
most famous ones that we’ve seen a hundred
times.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:18.830
But going deeper (and he forces himself to
go deeper) that is where you get the creativity

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:19.830
and originality.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:25.370
And eventually when you reach past the 10
to 12 or 15 all the way up to 20, you’ll

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:29.270
start getting really original ways because
you force yourself.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.530
And so that is what the writer needs to do.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.460
Force yourself to go deeper in order to create
some original writing.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:40.520
Film Courage: Right…so getting out all…probably
number 1 through 5 is just total clichés?

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:41.520
Karl Iglesias: Right.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:42.520
You can try it as a game.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:49.069
It’s a tip that he uses as a habit of creativity
to do the 20 for just anything.

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:51.990
It could be 20 different ways to rob a bank.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.610
20 different ways to say I love you.

00:02:55.610 --> 00:02:57.540
20 different ways to break up with someone.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.970
20 different ways to be mean to someone.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.550
There are all these different things that
you’ve seen a 100 times and when you see

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:07.510
it over and over again you go “Hmm…”
because you’ve seen it a 100 times and it’s

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:12.319
only the movies that break out…stories that
break out are the ones that are truly original,

00:03:12.319 --> 00:03:13.319
right?

00:03:13.319 --> 00:03:17.940
Film Courage: So let’s take the movie GREEN
CARD with Gérard Depardieu and Andie MacDowell.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.550
So that probably be further down the list,
I would think?

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:22.550
It’s a cute movie.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:23.550
It’s original.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:24.550
Karl Iglesias: Right.

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:25.550
It was an interesting movie.

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:29.110
That is the thing about what I love about
romantic comedies is that it’s such a set

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.920
story in terms of shape, in terms of structure.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.319
In terms of Boy-Gets-Girl or vice versa Girl-Gets-Boy,
Girl-Loses-Boy-Gets-Boy-In-The-End.

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:39.959
It’s the same thing.

00:03:39.959 --> 00:03:42.270
All romantic comedies are the same literally.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:48.130
But the ones that I think we pay attention
to and that are successful are the ones who

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:49.540
have the unique angle to it.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.060
[For instance] HOOK, same thing only different.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:58.020
The only different are the ones like GREEN
CARD or ETERNAL SUNSHINE OF THE SPOTLESS MIND

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:05.080
or even THE BREAK UP which is a romantic comedy
where they break up as opposed to getting

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:06.550
together.

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:12.260
So whenever you come up with a unique angle
to that story that we all know, the better

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:13.260
for you.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.730
That is where originality comes in.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:23.960
And it gets harder and harder because the
more romantic comedies there is, the harder

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.669
it is to come up with something new, you know?

00:04:25.669 --> 00:04:26.669
That’s the way it is.

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:28.729
Question for the viewers: Have you ever brainstormed
a list of 20 ideas?

00:04:28.729 --> 00:04:28.990
Is this something you will now try?

